esses either  growing or or testing or selling  marijuana and that that might be the  area where it's a little tricky but I'm  I'm not even not concerned about it  quite frankly because we've already seen  state governments issuing similar  licenses for medical marijuana  businesses and certainly in Colorado has  a thriving medical marijuana business  which I guess is one of the reasons why  people were comfortable with it we're  getting passive aggression from the feds  certainly Department of Transportation  Drug Enforcement Administration the drug  czars office they've all stated that  federal law is still the law the land  and this stuff you're in trouble if  you're monkeying around with this  president obama and and Eric Holder have  been silent on the issue what's your  sensor is the federal government going  to like fight this to the end the Obama  administration is going to be slightly  hostile but not terribly postle so on  the criminal side the federal government  does not have the resources to go after  any individuals caregivers businesses  etc in either state Washington State's  ballot initiative was somewhat  controversial among legalization  proponents in that it did specify  penalties for driving under the  influence of marijuana what is the way  that that's going to work out in terms  of being tested is there a sense of how  how will police know if somebody is high  while driving in Washington State it's  going to be similar to how it works with  alcohol in any state which is that the  cops aren't going to just start randomly  stopping drivers and taking their urine  or taking their blood to look for  alcohol or marijuana that you actually  have to be driving what the police would  say we'd be dangerously and then after  some field sobriety tests you know  touching your nose and walking the line  and so forth then after the police feel  like maybe you really are dangerous then  they could take you in for the for the  blood test you know did you ever really  seriously think that you would be seeing  states legalizing marijuana use by  ballot initiative in your lifetime yes  oh yeah I absolutely did are we behind  or ahead of yours well unfortunately the  American people are a little bit behind  Rob campuses scheduled by that but  things are on track yeah what did you do  to celebrate the legalization of  marijuana in Colorado and Washington so  I was actually in denver on election  night because we had a feeling that we  were going to win there and that was the  initiative that we were more financially  invested in and so my goal was to be  absolutely sober for all the interviews  i had to do  and then afterwards drinking a bit of  whiskey so that's my my personal  contribution to election night but I  have to tell you that it was a wonderful  feeling being in that that celebration  with people in Colorado who actually  lived there full time I only lived there  part time and just to see everyone just  going wild on on election night where  people didn't even really care about any  of the other elections it was the most  important election night celebration  I've had well that's great we'll leave  it there Rob Campea of marijuana policy  project thanks for the work that you've  done and congratulations on the success  of this for reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie
 

